17. Response contract

17.1 Envelope

Every authoritative external final response is one UTF-8 JSON file whose top-level object conforms to closed-loop-stage-response/3 and contains exactly:

- schema;
- contractProfileId;
- jobId;
- stage;
- operation;
- promptIdentity;
- packageId;
- operationReservationId;
- challengeNonce;
- scope;
- responseType;
- humanInputRequests;
- humanAuthorityCandidates;
- stageData;
- records;
- evidence;
- unresolved;
- warnings;
- attachments.

promptIdentity is one closed object containing instructionId, bodySha256, contractSha256, and contextSignature. Those values MUST NOT be duplicated at the top level. A compatibility import containing duplicated identity values is historical-only unless every duplicate equals the nested value byte-for-byte and an explicit migration rule removes the duplicates before current validation.

Stage-operation contracts determine the exact required, prohibited, and nullable scope fields. Unknown properties are rejected.

17.2 Prompt identity

promptIdentity.bodySha256 is the SHA-256 of the exact authoritative prompt-file bytes defined in Section 10.1.
promptIdentity.contractSha256 is the SHA-256 of the exact canonical current response contract.
promptIdentity.contextSignature is the SHA-256 of the exact canonical current scope and selected-context manifest.
promptIdentity.instructionId is application allocated under closed-loop-id/1.

No identity includes its own digest, the response bytes that later echo it, or another circularly dependent value.

17.3 Response types

Exactly these primary response types are allowed:
- DATA_PROPOSAL
- HUMAN_INPUT_REQUIRED
- BLOCKED
- EXECUTION_FAILED

They are mutually exclusive.
Clarification, blockers, and execution failures are controlled response events. They are not accepted canonical data changes.

17.4 Record identity

A proposed record uses exactly one of:
- tempKey for a response-local new proposal;
- targetId for an application-reserved current slot.

A targetId must:
- already exist;
- belong to the same project;
- belong to the correct stage and operation;
- belong to the current scope;
- permit completion or update;
- expose only agent-writable fields.

Humans and agents cannot allocate canonical IDs.

